      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="portfolio-grid case-study-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Project Overview</span>
              <h2>What the system does and why teams depended on it</h2>
              <p>UAgancy is a saas platform built for marketing and service agencies managing many client accounts and campaign cycles. The product was designed to replace scattered client, campaign, and reporting workflows with one coordinated operations platform.</p>
              <p>From an engineering perspective, the system had to support many client workspaces, recurring reporting cycles, and team coordination workflows without fragmentation. That made it a production platform with real operational demands rather than a portfolio demo.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Business Challenge</span>
              <h2>What had to improve for the product to scale cleanly</h2>
              <p>The core business challenge was delivering a product that could serve agency operations and service delivery software use cases while still remaining easy for operators to trust and use every day.</p>
              <p>The client needed better internal visibility and repeatability so account teams could spend more time on delivery and less on manual reporting assembly.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading compact-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Constraints &amp; Complexity</span>
              <h3>Engineering realities that shaped implementation choices</h3>
            </div>
            <p>The delivery had to solve product problems, operational risk, and scaling concerns at the same time.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="expertise-grid project-complexity-grid">
            
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Multi-Client Coordination</h3>
              <p>Different client accounts and campaign states needed clear boundaries and visibility.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Reporting Cadence</h3>
              <p>Recurring updates had to be produced more efficiently without losing important context.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Team Alignment</h3>
              <p>Operators, account managers, and leadership needed a shared view of delivery status.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Maintainability</h3>
              <p>Agency workflows change often, so the platform needed an adaptable architecture.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section section-accent">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Solution Architecture</span>
              <h2>System design, integrations, and delivery approach</h2>
            </div>
            <p>The implementation emphasized maintainability, production-readiness, and cleaner operational workflows.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="case-study-hero architecture-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <h3>Implementation approach</h3>
              <p>The solution used Laravel, React, Tailwind CSS, MySQL across a modular application architecture. Core workflows were separated into clearer domain boundaries so business logic, integrations, and UI concerns did not become tightly coupled.</p>
              <p>That approach made the system easier to scale, easier to monitor, and easier to extend as new requirements appeared. It also created better release confidence for a product expected to handle real customer traffic and operator activity.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel architecture-diagram-panel">
              <span class="eyebrow">Architecture Diagram</span>
              <div class="architecture-diagram" aria-label="System architecture">
                <div class="arch-node">Operator UI</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-node">Domain Services</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-row">
                  <div class="arch-node small">Integrations</div>
                  <div class="arch-node small">Reporting</div>
                </div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-node">Primary Data Store</div>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

